{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Pushed patch set 2 of change 1717 (commit 4fbbda329f) with all 5 review findings fixed.\n\nTriaged the ps1 Verified -1 before pushing, per the playbook. Failure was tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/diffing/test_inbound_removal_confirmation_a4bd.py::test_pass_summary_reports_the_decline_count on ONE shard (macos-latest py3.13); ubuntu 3.11/3.12/3.13 passed the identical code. It is NOT mine and NOT covered by my amendment — category (b), a known order-dependent test with a fix in flight.\n\nEvidence, in the order I got it:\n- My diff touches compact.py, compact-sweep.yml, docs/concurrency.md and compaction/workflow tests. Nothing reaches the reconciler.\n- The test passes in ISOLATION on macOS, the same platform that failed: 10/10.\n- Running the whole tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/ directory alone passes too (3 random orderings). So the trigger is cross-directory state, not anything within that suite.\n- My own full main tier reproduced it (1 failed, 12715 passed) — so I could study it locally rather than argue from CI logs.\n- DECISIVE: git log on that test file returns exactly one commit, 4010da6493 'Decline removals of never-confirmed peer links', dated 2026-08-12 — one of the 8 commits I rebased onto. The file is brand new. It cannot have been broken by a change that post-dates it, and my earlier pre-rebase full run (12665 passed, 0 failed) did not contain it at all.\n\nMechanism, for whoever holds the in-flight fix: the test asserts a once-per-pass INFO summary ('declined N inbound link removal(s)', apply_inbound_records.py:792) reaches caplog. The captured records contain the PER-LINK decline message from the same module logger, so logger propagation is intact — the summary simply was not emitted, which points at the  counter rather than at log capture. Adding tests elsewhere in the suite reshuffles pytest-randomly's ordering, which is why an unrelated change surfaces it.\n\nI did NOT recheck this one, because a recheck re-runs the same order-dependent test and the fix is already in flight; ps2 gets a fresh CI run anyway, and if it trips again the answer is that fix landing, not a retry from me.\n\nLocal state on ps2: main tier 12715 passed with only that known flake failing; integration tier 194 passed, 1 skipped; gates all green. Waiting on ps2 votes."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786612750693746001,"uuid":"61d59a4d-ef2f-48aa-bfe5-eb53809b8b59"}